+callback, preserving prior behavior.
+
+The mutable callback receives only each declared logical state span: the
+headerless primary payload or the child's bytes after its 128-byte account
+header, bounded to the length committed when that span was created. It does not
+receive the session, resource, view-output, or publication-scratch accounts as
+mutable state. It receives the session-bound workspace payload as a separate
+bounded mutable slice and the current output chunk as a mutable slice. The
+processor publishes no view-output account until `COMMIT_PHASE`.
+
+This is an account-and-slice boundary, not a per-byte write monitor inside a
+state span. An opted-in kernel can write anywhere within every declared state
+span it receives; its application contract must list the exact state byte
+ranges it may change and when. The generic processor does not verify that
+byte-level allowlist. Application acceptance therefore requires an actual
+program test that snapshots state around rendering, rejects any changed byte
+outside the declared ranges, and verifies outputs remain unpublished until
+commit. A successful phase commits its workspace and state writes; a later
+phase refusal rolls back that later transaction only, not earlier successful
+phases. The workspace is session-bound and versioned alongside the state
+cursor.
 
